 目录                    │\n    ├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    │        Agents（角色层）                 │  定义做什么（职责范围）\n    │    plugins-*/*/agents/ 目录             │\n    ├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    │        Skills（能力层）                 │  定义怎么做（具体实现）\n    │    {领域}/ 目录下的各 Skill 子目录       │\n    ├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    │        References（知识层）             │  定义如何做得更好\n    │    内嵌在 Skills 中的最佳实践           │\n    ├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n    │    Infrastructure（基础设施层）         │  提供底层工具和文档支持\n    │    infra/ 目录下的工具类 Skill          │\n    └─────────────────────────────────────────┘\n         ↑ 效果评测横向覆盖所有层级 ↑\n    `\n\n> 注：领域指业务领域目录，如 `ops/`（算子）、`model/`（模型）、`graph/`（图编译）、`runtime/`（运行时）、`tools/`（CANN 工具链）等\n\n## 项目结构\n    \n    `cannbot-skills/\n    ├── ops/                      # 算子 Skills（正式版），每个子目录即一个 Skill\n    │   ├── ascendc-api-best-practices/\n    │   ├── ascendc-tiling-design/\n    │   ├── ops-simulator/\n    │   └── ...                   # 各 Skill 目录\n    ├── model/                    # 模型推理优化 Skills，每个子目录即一个 Skill\n    │   ├── model-infer-fusion/\n    │   ├── model-infer-quantization/\n    │   └── ...                   # 各 Skill 目录\n    ├── graph/                    # 图编译 Skills\n    ├── runtime/                  # 运行时迁移 Skills\n    │   └── runtime_migration/\n    ├── infra/                    # 基础设施 Skills（跨领域工具）\n    │   ├── gitcode-toolkit/\n    │   └── ...\n    ├── tools/                    # CANN 工具链 Skills（工具使用指导）\n    │   ├── asys-toolkit/\n    │   ├── msaicerr-toolkit/\n    │   ├── msnpureport-toolkit/\n    │   └── tool-npu-compute/\n    ├── plugins-official/         # 官方 Plugin（Plugin 配置 + Agents + init.sh）\n    │   ├── ops-registry-invoke/  # 算子开发 Plugin（示例）\n    │   │   ├── agents/           # Agent 定义（.md）\n    │   │   ├── workflow/         # 工作流 Skill\n    │   │   ├── AGENTS.md         # Plugin 配置\n    │   │   ├── init.sh           # 依赖安装脚本\n    │   │   └── quickstart.md     # 快速入门\n    │   ├── model-infer-optimize/\n    │   ├── ops-direct-invoke/\n    │   └── ...                   # 各官方 Plugin\n    ├── plugins-community/        # 社区 Plugin（实验/非正式版）\n    ├── tests/                    # 测试框架与用例\n    ├── docs/                     # 项目规范文档\n    │   └── STANDARDS.md          # 开发规范总集\n    ├── install.sh                # 一键安装入口\n    ├── AGENTS.md                 # 本文件\n    ├── CHANGELOG.md              # 变更日志\n    ├── LICENSE                   # 开源许可证\n    ├── OAT.xml                   # 开源审计\n    └── README.md                 # 项目说明\n    `\n\n## Skills 分类\n\n项目 skills 目录按功能分类，包括：\n\n  * 知识库类：提供领域知识和参考资料\n  * 工程模板类：提供工程脚手架和项目模板\n  * 调试与测试类：问题诊断和调试\n  * 测试开发类：测试设计、测试开发和代码检视\n  * 工具辅助类：辅助工具和实用功能\n\n详细分类见 Skills 开发规范\n\n## 详细规范\n\n详见 开发规范总集--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond the Benchmark Shape: Evaluating AI-Generated CUDA and Triton Kernels Under Realistic AI Workloads | Deaimer Research (https://www.deaimer.com/research/beyond-the-benchmark-shape-evaluating-ai-generated-cuda-and--0041e838)\nciteturn33search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: yesterday; Crawled: today; Large language models and coding agents can increasingly write GPU kernels, but a kernel that compiles, agrees with a reference on familiar inputs, and wins a timing test has not necessarily become a safe or economically meaningful replacement in an AI system. ... AI Agent Benchmarks and Evaluation Research (public benchmark principles), accessed October 19, 2026. https://www.deaimer.com/benchmarks\n\nBenchmarks Paper\n\n# Beyond the Benchmark Shape: Evaluating AI-Generated CUDA and Triton Kernels Under Realistic AI Workloads\n\nOct 19, 2026\n\nBy Muhammad Sarmad\n\nPrefer to read offline?Download the full research as a PDF.\n\nDownload PDF\n\nImage: Beyond the Benchmark Shape: Evaluating AI-Generated CUDA and Triton Kernels Under Realistic AI Workloads\n\n## Abstract\n\nLarge language models and coding agents can increasingly write GPU kernels, but a kernel that compiles, agrees with a reference on familiar inputs, and wins a timing test has not necessarily become a safe or economically meaningful replacement in an AI system. Performance depends on tensor shape, numerical format, memory layout, compiler behavior, GPU generation, launch mechanism, and the surrounding training or inference workload. Recent work has made important progress: KernelBench established executable kernel-generation evaluation; KernelBench-X documented category-dependent correctness and hardware-efficiency gaps; SOL-ExecBench introduced hardware-oriented performance bounds; Atrex-Bench incorporated trace-derived workload weights; and FastKernels studied production-oriented integration [6–10]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncannbot-skills-2026-10-01-002-contrib/docs · LiJianhao2/cannbot-skills-2026-10-01-002-contrib - AtomGit (https://gitcode.com/LiJianhao2/cannbot-skills-2026-10-01-002-contrib/tree/master/docs)\nciteturn33search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; CANNBot Skills 项目文档索引。\nmaster\n\n\nREADME\n\n# 文档导航\n\nCANNBot Skills 项目文档索引。各文档的入口与推荐阅读顺序如下。\n\n## 文档列表\n\n文档  | 内容  | 适用读者\n--- | --- | ---\n安装指南  | 安装命令、使用入门、故障排查  | 所有用户\n功能清单  | 全量 Skills/Agents 清单  | 所有用户\n使用样例  | 各 Skill 可复制的 prompt 样例  | 所有用户\n架构设计  | 三层架构与各开发路径逻辑视图  | 贡献者\n开发规范  | 命名、结构、分类体系、代码规范  | 贡献者\n贡献指南  | 贡献流程、准入门槛、评审规则  | 贡献者\n治理规范  | 成文法+判例法模型、角色职责  | Committer / 贡献者\n\n## 推荐阅读顺序\n\n  * 新用户：安装指南 → 功能清单 → 使用样例\n  * 快速评估者：README → 功能清单 → 架构设计\n  * 贡献者：功能清单 → 架构设计 → 开发规范 → 贡献指南\n  * Committer：贡献指南 → 治理规范\n\n* * *--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCAKE from Scratch: Agents Write GPU Kernels, and the Compiler Learns from Their Mistakes | Gang Liao (https://gangliao.me/blog/2026/cake-deep-dive/)\nciteturn33search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; October 10, 2026 · 65 min read ...   * Part I · The Machine (§1–4): what a GPU kernel is, why speed is mostly about moving data, how modern GPUs turned into asynchronous assembly lines, and what separates an expert’s kernel from a merely correct one. ... Before any aggregate is reported, validation covers representative and held-out inputs, boundary and tail cases, overlapping or missing guards, and the fallback path; the shape set is fixed before tuning, and guards may split it but not add evaluation shapes.\n# CAKE from Scratch: Agents Write GPU Kernels, and the Compiler Learns from Their Mistakes\n\nOctober 10, 2026 · 65 min read\n\nEnglish 中文\n\n#### Contents\n\nIn 2022, FlashAttention made the attention layer of a transformer much faster on the same GPU, without approximating anything: it computes exactly the same attention formula, just in a different order, so its results differ from the usual method only by floating-point rounding. It did not find a new formula. It found a better schedule: which piece of data sits in which memory, which part of the chip works on it, and in what order. Three more versions followed, each pairing a better schedule with algorithmic changes suited to the hardware of its day; the last two were written for new GPU generations.\n\nPrograms like these are called GPU kernels, and they are where much of the money in AI is spent: every token a large language model (LLM) reads or writes passes through dozens of them. Lately, AI coding agents have started to write kernels too. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI-written GPU kernels: PTXBench grades them, and an agent gamed the grade with the constant 0.1778209953 (https://ai.thesatyajit.com/articles/ptxbench-kda-kernels)\nciteturn33search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; 2026-10-02 · 15 min · agents · kernels · gpu · cuda · benchmarks · linear-attention · explainer ... PTXBench (arXiv 2608.17379, v3, 2026-09-28; code github.com/zhang677/PTXBench, Apache-2.0) builds the measurement: make models write CUDA with inline PTX from scratch, ban the vendor libraries, and check whether the kernel is correct, whether the requested instructions actually executed, and whether it beats the library.\n\n# AI-written GPU kernels: PTXBench grades them, and an agent gamed the grade with the constant 0.1778209953\n\n2026-10-02 · 15 min · agents · kernels · gpu · cuda · benchmarks · linear-attention · explainer\n\nWriting a fast attention or GEMM kernel for a current GPU is not a matter of writing good C++. The speed lives below CUDA C: in architecture-specific instructions that drive the tensor cores, stage data through the Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA), park accumulators in the new tensor memory (TMEM) on Blackwell, and issue warpgroup matrix multiplies (WGMMA) at the right tile shape. FlashAttention, MLA, and Kimi Delta Attention are fast because someone hand-placed those instructions. So the obvious question for 2026: can a model do that?\n\nTwo results this autumn answer it from opposite ends. PTXBench (arXiv 2608.17379, v3, 2026-09-28; code github.com/zhang677/PTXBench, Apache-2.0) builds the measurement: make models write CUDA with inline PTX from scratch, ban the vendor libraries, and check whether the kernel is correct, whether the requested instructions actually executed, and whether it beats the library. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlashInfer Autotuner v2: Tune the Way You Serve | FlashInfer (https://flashinfer.ai/2026/09/22/autotuner-v2.html)\nciteturn33search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 weeks ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; Sep 22, 2026 • Yang Xu, Albert Cheng, Vincent Tombari, Alex Yang, Brian K. ... How FlashInfer rebuilt GPU kernel autotuning for better production LLM serving at scale. ... Benchmark methodology: beyond single-kernel timing.\n\n# FlashInfer Autotuner v2: Tune the Way You Serve\n\nSep 22, 2026 • Yang Xu, Albert Cheng, Vincent Tombari, Alex Yang, Brian K. Ryu, Jingfan Sun, Lee Nau, Xin Li, and Po-Han Huang\n\nHow FlashInfer rebuilt GPU kernel autotuning for better production LLM serving at scale.\n\nFlashInfer v0.7 introduces Autotuner v2, a redesign of how FlashInfer measures, persists, and reuses GPU-kernel choices. The same operation can often run through several backends, algorithms, or configurations, which FlashInfer calls tactics. Because the fastest tactic changes with shape and GPU, the autotuner measures candidates during warmup and reuses the winner during execution.\n\nThe legacy v1 design treated GPU device time as the ranking objective. That proxy works when omitted host overhead is negligible or nearly equal across tactics, but execution mode can break the assumption. Eager execution repeats framework and backend launch work on every call; under CUDA Graph execution, the launch sequence is captured once, so tuning ranks replay latency. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPTXBench: A new way to measure how well LLMs can optimize GPU kernels (https://brainszone.ai/blog/ptxbench-llm-gpu-kernel-optimization)\nciteturn33search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; 3 September 2026 0 viewsThe benchmark evaluates models on GEMM and attention tasks for H100 and B200 GPUs — from correctness to real speedup. ... GPU kernel optimization usually requires a deep understanding of hardware architecture and low-level instructions.\n\n# PTXBench: A new way to measure how well LLMs can optimize GPU kernels\n\n3 September 2026 0 views\n\nThe benchmark evaluates models on GEMM and attention tasks for H100 and B200 GPUs — from correctness to real speedup. Results show that even precise execution of target instructions does not guarantee competitive performance.\n\nImage: PTXBench: A new way to measure how well LLMs can optimize GPU kernels\n\nContents\n\n  1. What is PTXBench and why is it needed\n  2. What the benchmark measures\n  3. Results: optimization is still uneven across models\n  4. How the model was fine-tuned and what worked\n  5. Key takeaway\n\nGPU kernel optimization usually requires a deep understanding of hardware architecture and low-level instructions. Language models still handle this inconsistently: they can generate plausible code, but it is far from always correct and fast. PTXBench is a new benchmark that tries to bring measurability and clarity to this process.\n\n## What is PTXBench and why is it needed\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNVIDIA Announced Native Rust GPU Kernels. The Two-Track Design Is the Most Honest Thing They Could Have Done. - ThesisBench (https://thesisbench.com/nvidia-announced-native-rust-gpu-kernels-the-two-track-design-is-the-most-honest-thing-they-could-have-done/)\nciteturn33search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Image: NVIDIA Announced Native Rust GPU Kernels. ... NVIDIA published the CUDA Rust announcement on September 8, 2026, and the Hacker News thread hit 800 points before most people had finished reading the blog post.\n\nDeveloper Tools\n\n# NVIDIA Announced Native Rust GPU Kernels. The Two-Track Design Is the Most Honest Thing They Could Have Done.\n\nImage: Michael Sun By Michael Sun · September 17, 2026 · 19 min read\n\nImage: NVIDIA Announced Native Rust GPU Kernels. The Two-Track Design Is the Most Honest Thing They Could Have Done.\n\n## NVIDIA Announced Native Rust GPU Kernels. The Two-Track Design Is the Most Honest Thing They Could Have Done.\n\nNVIDIA published the CUDA Rust announcement on September 8, 2026, and the Hacker News thread hit 800 points before most people had finished reading the blog post. The reaction was predictable: a mix of genuine excitement about memory safety on GPUs and the usual skepticism about whether a vendor-backed Rust project would ever reach the quality of the C++ toolchain it sits beside. Both reactions are partially right and missing the more interesting question, which is why NVIDIA chose two separate projects instead of one.\n\nI have been writing GPU kernels since 2017, first in CUDA C++ for computer vision pipelines, then in Triton for attention variants, and most recently evaluating whether any of the Rust GPU tooling was production-ready for inference serving infrastructure. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGenerated-Code and GPU-Kernel Correctness Research | Dipankar Sarkar (https://www.dipankar.cc/research/generated-code/)\nciteturn33search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago;   * Your claim is about a compressed or distilled model rather than generated code — see efficient inference and distillation evaluation, which is an open research direction rather than a published result. ... 2026 · Preprint (not peer reviewed) · cited by 6 (Google Scholar, October 2026) ...   * gpuemu-corpus (GitHub) — 26 ops (16 correct controls, 10 seeded buggy variants), drivers for the four kernel papers, stored seeds and replay scripts.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) KernelOPT: Dispatch-Aware Agentic Search for GPU Kernel Optimization (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/414739448_KernelOPT_Dispatch-Aware_Agentic_Search_for_GPU_Kernel_Optimization)\nciteturn33search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: last week;   * September 2026 ... Deep learning inference and training performance depends critically on GPU kernel efficiency.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK/main/README.md)\nciteturn33view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AMD-AGI/GEAK/main/README.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 253\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alibaba/atrex-kernel-agent/v0.3.0/README.md)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alibaba/atrex-kernel-agent/v0.3.0/README.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 117\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn33view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gitcode.com/cann/cannbot-skills/-/raw/master/CHANGELOG.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn33view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://gitcode.com/cann/cannbot-skills/-/raw/main/CHANGELOG.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
